THIRD-PARTY LIBRARIES
=====================

dlt — Apache License 2.0
  https://github.com/dlt-hub/dlt
  Code-first data-injection library; used for the ETL layer instead of hand-rolling
  schema inference, incremental loading, and retries.
  Status: adopted. Pinned to dlt>=1.27.2; 1.27.0 and 1.27.1 were yanked by the
  maintainers over a data-loss bug in incremental merge, which is the exact operation
  this pipeline performs. The pin is enforced by a test.
  Apache-2.0 obligations: the dlt copyright notice and license text ship with the
  dependency and are reproduced by its own distribution. This project links dlt
  unmodified and makes no changes to it, so there is nothing further to state here. If
  we ever vendor or patch dlt, this file must record that and describe the change.

Crawl4AI — Apache License 2.0
  https://github.com/unclecode/crawl4ai
  LLM-ready web crawler. Considered for browser-rendering of JS-heavy listing pages.
  Status: referenced only, NOT a dependency of v1. Every source we ingest returns JSON
  over plain HTTP, so there is no JavaScript page to render. Adopting it now would buy
  a browser download in CI and nothing else. The seam is reserved for a future HTML
  detail-page enrichment path that would raise pay coverage, and is adopted only if a
  measured sample of target pages justifies it.
  Apache-2.0 obligations: none are triggered by v1, because the code is not
  redistributed here. If it is added later, its notice and license ship with the
  dependency and any local modification must be described in this file.

Better Auth — MIT License
  https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth
  Framework-agnostic authentication library for TypeScript. Used for email and
  password authentication, self-hosted, with its tables in the same libSQL database as
  the rest of the app.
  Status: adopted, pending implementation.
  MIT obligations: the copyright notice and license text ship with the dependency and
  are reproduced by its own distribution. This project uses it unmodified.


DATA SOURCE ATTRIBUTION
=======================

Remote OK — public API, https://remoteok.com/api
  Every response from the Remote OK API carries the publisher's terms inside the first
  object's `legal` field, which reads:

    "Please link back (with follow, and without nofollow!) to the URL on Remote OK and
     mention Remote OK as a source, so we get traffic back from your site. If you do
     not we'll have to suspend API access. Please don't use the Remote OK logo without
     written permission."

  That is a condition of access, and this project accepts it. Concretely, the deployed
  application must:

    1. display a visible "Remote OK" attribution link pointing at remoteok.com on every
       page, with rel="follow" and no rel="nofollow";
    2. name Remote OK as a source wherever posting data is shown; and
    3. not render the Remote OK logo.

  The notice is preserved as data rather than discarded: the source module keeps the
  `legal` object verbatim instead of dropping it during parsing, so the obligation stays
  visible in the stored payload and the pipeline cannot silently erase it.

  Jobicy (https://jobicy.com) and Himalayas (https://himalayas.app) are ingested under
  the same public, no-key, no-login rule and are attributed on the coverage page. No
  platform requiring login to a personal account is scraped by this project.
